1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,649
On a summer night in 1958,

2
00:00:02,649 --> 00:00:09,121
the water in this bay climbed five hundred
and twenty-four metres up a mountainside.

3
00:00:09,870 --> 00:00:15,488
Higher than the Empire State Building.
The highest wave run-up ever recorded.

4
00:00:18,736 --> 00:00:25,663
Lituya Bay is a narrow fjord on the remote
coast of southeast Alaska, carved by glaciers.

5
00:00:25,663 --> 00:00:29,510
A long gravel spit almost closes its mouth.

6
00:00:30,081 --> 00:00:32,210
At its head, the bay forms a T.

7
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:40,953
The crossbar is a trench along the Fairweather Fault, where
the Pacific and North American plates grind past each other.

8
00:00:41,345 --> 00:00:44,777
Glaciers flow down into both arms of the T.

9
00:00:46,062 --> 00:00:51,206
On the ninth of July, three fishing
boats lay at anchor near the entrance.

10
00:00:51,206 --> 00:00:59,144
It was after ten at night, but this far north in
midsummer, the sun had only just set. The sky stayed pale.

11
00:00:59,893 --> 00:01:03,459
On the Edrie, Howard Ulrich
and his young son, Sonny.

12
00:01:03,459 --> 00:01:09,477
On the Badger, Bill and Vivian Swanson.
On the Sunmore, Orville and Mickey Wagner.

13
00:01:11,118 --> 00:01:15,090
At about a quarter past ten,
the Fairweather Fault ruptured.

14
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:18,304
A magnitude seven point eight earthquake.

15
00:01:19,054 --> 00:01:22,579
At the head of the bay, in
an arm called Gilbert Inlet,

16
00:01:22,579 --> 00:01:26,155
the shaking broke loose
part of a mountain wall.

17
00:01:26,547 --> 00:01:29,768
About thirty million
cubic metres of rock fell,

18
00:01:29,768 --> 00:01:34,448
from as high as nine hundred
metres, straight into the water.

19
00:01:35,911 --> 00:01:41,477
The impact drove the water of the inlet
across to the opposite shore. And up.

20
00:01:41,869 --> 00:01:45,434
It tore up the facing slope,
through old spruce forest,

21
00:01:45,434 --> 00:01:49,240
to a height of five hundred
and twenty-four metres.

22
00:01:49,811 --> 00:01:53,387
Trees were snapped off or
ripped out by the roots.

23
00:01:53,387 --> 00:01:58,393
In places, the soil itself was
scoured away, down to bare rock.

24
00:02:00,034 --> 00:02:05,656
To picture that height: the Eiffel Tower
stands three hundred and thirty metres tall.

25
00:02:05,656 --> 00:02:11,646
The Empire State Building, to the tip of
its antenna, four hundred and forty-three.

26
00:02:12,217 --> 00:02:17,461
The water went higher than both. Around
eighty metres higher than the antenna.

27
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:20,800
It's worth being precise.

28
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:28,293
Five hundred and twenty-four metres is the run-up:
how high the water reached on land, above sea level.

29
00:02:28,685 --> 00:02:36,467
The wave itself was lower. Laboratory models suggest roughly
a hundred and fifty metres where it struck the slope.

30
00:02:36,467 --> 00:02:39,971
Its momentum carried it the rest of the way.

31
00:02:41,077 --> 00:02:43,374
And it was not a classic tsunami.

32
00:02:43,374 --> 00:02:50,626
Those are usually born when an earthquake heaves
the sea floor, and they can cross entire oceans.

33
00:02:51,018 --> 00:02:56,616
This was a giant splash. A landslide,
slamming into a narrow, enclosed bay.

34
00:02:56,616 --> 00:03:02,640
Catastrophic inside it. Outside, it
spread out and quickly lost its power.

35
00:03:04,104 --> 00:03:08,223
From the head of the bay, the
wave raced toward the entrance.

36
00:03:08,223 --> 00:03:15,273
Still tens of metres high. By some estimates,
faster than a hundred and fifty kilometres an hour.

37
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:17,933
Howard Ulrich saw it coming.

38
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:22,147
He let out his anchor chain and
turned the Edrie to face it.

39
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:29,679
The wave lifted the boat, snapped the chain, and threw
it toward the shore, then swept it back into the bay.

40
00:03:29,679 --> 00:03:31,765
Father and son survived.

41
00:03:32,514 --> 00:03:36,216
The Badger was carried right
over the spit, out to sea.

42
00:03:36,216 --> 00:03:42,775
It sank. The Swansons escaped in a small skiff
and were picked up by another fishing boat.

43
00:03:43,524 --> 00:03:46,771
The Sunmore had tried to
run for the open ocean.

44
00:03:46,771 --> 00:03:51,397
It was never seen again. Orville
and Mickey Wagner were lost.

45
00:03:53,574 --> 00:04:00,398
Soon after, the geologist Don Miller of the
U.S. Geological Survey photographed the bay.

46
00:04:00,398 --> 00:04:07,334
Its shores were ringed by a sharp line:
forest above, raw ground below. A trimline.

47
00:04:07,905 --> 00:04:09,889
He found older trimlines too.

48
00:04:09,889 --> 00:04:16,000
Giant waves had swept the bay at least
four times in a little more than a century.

49
00:04:17,106 --> 00:04:25,473
Decades later, scientists rebuilt Gilbert Inlet as a
laboratory model, at one six-hundred-and-seventy-fifth scale.

50
00:04:25,473 --> 00:04:30,157
Its model wave ran up to the
height of the real trimline.

51
00:04:30,549 --> 00:04:35,276
Computer simulations since then
have reproduced the run-up too.

52
00:04:36,918 --> 00:04:39,600
And the danger is not only in the past.

53
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:45,602
As glaciers thin and retreat, they can
leave steep valley walls without support.

54
00:04:46,172 --> 00:04:46,817
In 2015,

55
00:04:46,817 --> 00:04:54,660
in Taan Fiord, Alaska, a landslide sent water
a hundred and ninety-three metres up a slope.

56
00:04:55,231 --> 00:05:04,159
In Barry Arm, in Prince William Sound, a slowly creeping slope
of hundreds of millions of cubic metres hangs above a fjord.

57
00:05:04,159 --> 00:05:12,207
A worst-case model suggests run-up of more than five
hundred metres. Scientists are watching it closely.

58
00:05:14,206 --> 00:05:16,795
In Lituya Bay, the forest has grown back.

59
00:05:16,795 --> 00:05:20,768
But the line is still there,
if you know where to look.

60
00:05:21,695 --> 00:05:26,118
A mark on the mountain, five
hundred and twenty-four metres up.

61
00:05:26,689 --> 00:05:29,000
The height of one night's water.

